1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:07,370
My name is Doug Parkinson, and I'm the SLS Launch Integration and Mission Operations Lead.

2
00:00:07,370 --> 00:00:13,370
My job on SLS is to manage the operations architecture on the SLS program

3
00:00:13,370 --> 00:00:20,370
and specifically provide support in the SLS Engineering Support Center, the SESC.

4
00:00:20,370 --> 00:00:25,370
One of the specific things that we do is support launch operations.

5
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:30,370
One, two, one, and lift off of Artemis I.

6
00:00:30,370 --> 00:00:37,370
I was born in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. I lived in St. Louis for a little while, and then a small town in Tennessee.

7
00:00:37,370 --> 00:00:43,370
And I ended up landing here in Huntsville, Alabama, going to the University of Alabama in Huntsville.

8
00:00:43,370 --> 00:00:48,370
When I was a young man, I knew I'd be an engineer. My father was an engineer.

9
00:00:48,370 --> 00:00:50,370
I had the engineering knack, if you will.

10
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:55,370
For the time in the late 90s, I was looking at doing computer technologies.

11
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:58,370
So computer engineering was hot at the time.

12
00:00:58,370 --> 00:01:04,370
But when I got to school, I did feel like I was more drawn towards the automotive industry,

13
00:01:04,370 --> 00:01:07,370
and I thought that's where I might land one day.

14
00:01:07,370 --> 00:01:11,370
I did come across an opportunity to work at the Propulsion Research Center.

15
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:18,370
That intrigued me because it was a way, as an undergraduate, to put into practical use

16
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:20,370
what I was learning in books.

17
00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:26,370
I got my hands dirty, and it was in cutting-edge technologies that really inspired me.

18
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:28,370
And that led me to NASA.

19
00:01:28,370 --> 00:01:31,370
I've been with NASA since 1999.

20
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:36,370
So it's been a bit over 25 years now, and it's been quite a ride.

21
00:01:36,370 --> 00:01:41,370
In 2012, I transitioned over to the program office.

22
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:47,370
And one of the things that I learned in the propulsion area with all of the engine testing

23
00:01:47,370 --> 00:01:53,370
and, like, test operations in that translated well into my new role

24
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:58,370
as operations lead for stages element in the SLS program.

25
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:01,370
I love all aspects of the operations.

26
00:02:01,370 --> 00:02:03,370
I like getting my hands dirty.

27
00:02:03,370 --> 00:02:07,370
I like seeing the erector set go together with SLS.

28
00:02:07,370 --> 00:02:09,370
We are putting vehicles together.

29
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:12,370
On Artemis One, I was the operations lead,

30
00:02:12,370 --> 00:02:17,370
and I was able to establish the operations architecture for SLS,

31
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:22,370
which I hadn't ever done before, so that was a great new experience for me.

32
00:02:22,370 --> 00:02:27,370
And I was right here in the SESC, the SLS Engineering Support Center,

33
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:33,370
and I was supporting and watching on those screens behind me the Artemis One rocket launch.

34
00:02:33,370 --> 00:02:36,370
It means a lot to be a part of this Artemis team.

35
00:02:36,370 --> 00:02:42,370
I'm surrounded by a wonderful set of engineers that are all around me each and every day.

36
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:44,370
What a blessing.

37
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:49,370
And the technologies that we are developing, the missions that we have,

38
00:02:49,370 --> 00:02:54,370
the dreams that we have, it means a lot to show the next generation

39
00:02:54,370 --> 00:02:59,370
my children, kids that I coach, all of those kids are inspired.

40
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:00,370
I see it every day.

41
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:02,370
They ask me a lot of questions.

42
00:03:02,370 --> 00:03:07,370
They're just inspired by what we're doing and interested in seeing what may come.

